  "summary": "The middle phase of Gothic architecture emphasizing ornate decoration and intricate tracery patterns.",
  "description": "It features elaborate window designs with radiating patterns and increased use of stained glass. This style developed as builders focused more on decorative elements while maintaining structural innovations.",
  "culturalContext": "Rayonnant Gothic — the court style of Louis IX's Paris from the 1230s to mid-1300s — thinned structure toward glass: Sainte-Chapelle's walls nearly vanished into windows, Notre-Dame's transept facades opened into rose lattices, and radiating tracery gave the style its name. Architect Pierre de Montreuil led the key works; the style spread to León and Cologne before Flamboyant superseded it.",
